## Generalized gamma regression

The four standard AFT distributions each impose a fixed hazard shape. Picking among them by AIC
works, but it treats the candidates as unrelated. The generalized gamma distribution offers a more
principled approach: it is a three-parameter family that nests Weibull (Q = 1), log-normal (Q = 0),
and exponential (Q = 1, sigma = 1) as special cases. Fitting it gives a formal likelihood ratio test
of whether a simpler sub-model is adequate. The log-logistic isn't nested in it, so compare that one
by AIC.

::: {#f13661b8 .cell execution_count=23}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Fit a generalized gamma AFT model
lung = gw.load_dataset("lung", backend="polars")

gg = gw.AFT(dist="gengamma").fit("Surv(time, status) ~ age + sex", data=lung)
gg
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=23}
```
AFT (accelerated failure time model, dist='gengamma')

                 coef  se(coef)       z         p
(Intercept)     6.271    0.4854  12.919  3.52e-38
age          -0.01232  0.007045  -1.749   0.08027
sex            0.3842    0.1317   2.917  0.003529

Scale = 0.7575
Q = 0.9858
n = 228, events = 165
Log-likelihood = -1147
```
:::
:::


The extra shape parameter Q controls the distributional family. When Q is near 1 the data favor a
Weibull shape. When it is near 0 the data favor a log-normal shape. Values far from both suggest
that neither simple family is adequate. Here Q is close to 1, with a standard error of about 0.2, so
the lung data look Weibull.

### Testing nested sub-models

The `test_distributions()` method fits each nested sub-model (Weibull, log-normal, and exponential)
and compares it against the generalized gamma via a likelihood ratio test. The test has one degree
of freedom for the Weibull and the log-normal, which each fix Q, and two for the exponential, which
also fixes the scale. A large p-value means the simpler model cannot be rejected.

::: {#5dd4155f .cell execution_count=24}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Compare the generalized gamma against its nested sub-models
gg.test_distributions(format="polars")
```

