:::
:::


The coefficients are on a different scale now: `exp(coef)` is an odds ratio, not a hazard ratio.
A coefficient of `-0.89` for `sex`, for instance, means the odds of having died by any given time
are `exp(-0.89) ≈ 0.41` times as high for that group, holding age fixed, rather than the hazard
being scaled by that factor. Everything else works exactly as before: `predict()`,
`predict_quantile()`, `predict_expectation()`, `tidy()`, and `glance()` all behave the same way,
just evaluated on the odds scale internally.

::: {#e3883efc .cell execution_count=42}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Predicted survival at 6 months, 1 year, and 2 years from the proportional-odds model
rp_odds.predict(newdata, type="survival", times=[180, 365, 730], format="polars")
```

